cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite the manuscript; this repository supplies its source and verification artifacts."
type: software
title: "The thermodynamics of deadlines: one law for the work cost of acting in time"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-27
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
abstract: >-
  A quantum channel says what happens to each input, not when inputs arrive, when outputs leave for good, or
  what must be handed back. We show that for every finite schedule in which each carrier returns with its
  Hamiltonian and the controller comes back, the optimal work per use at vanishing error is the least entropy
  one implementation must add to its own record, plus the energy it hands over, against the most demanding
  observer. Landauer's bound, the thermodynamic capacity of Faist, Berta and Brandão in the time-covariant case,
  and the real-time costs of channels and processes are this law on different schedules. The cost survives
  arbitrarily slow operation: order, not speed, sets it. Releasing each output of a repeated memoryless channel
  before the next input arrives costs nothing extra for classical channels, a positive amount for partial reset
  at every temperature, and k_B T ln 3 per use for the Werner–Holevo qutrit at zero Hamiltonian, which is
  essentially free in batch. For a classical task with zero Hamiltonians that releases a past and later answers
  a question about it, the real-time and held prices are logarithms of two fiber counts; for reversible
  questions they differ by the logarithm of the number of classes of pasts that later questions tell apart. With
  a returned clock, the batch cost of every channel is a single-letter quantity, equal to the capacity when the
  environment can carry the channel's energy change; otherwise the clock's spread must grow at least linearly
  with the number of uses. Freeness is decidable in batch for exact algebraic data and undecidable in real time,
  already in a fixed dimension. A two-qubit process that is free when held costs every early-release device at
  least 0.87 k_B T ln 2 per episode at one percent error and budgets of 0.025 k_B T ln 2; for a classical
  version we propose a three-arm feedback-trap protocol.
keywords:
  - "quantum thermodynamics"
  - "time order"
  - "Landauer principle"
  - "thermodynamic capacity"
  - "quantum channels"
  - "quantum clocks"
  - "release rank"
  - "resource theories"
